    title: "Figurative language: similes, metaphors and idioms",
    key: "Literal language means exactly what the words say; figurative language means more. A *simile* compares with *like* or *as* (*as light as a feather*), a *metaphor* says one thing *is* another (*Time is money*), and an *idiom* is a fixed phrase whose meaning you have to learn (*break the ice*).",
    intro: "Figurative language isn't only for poems: it fills headlines, office talk and everyday conversation. Learners often understand every word in *It's a piece of cake* and still miss the meaning. Knowing the main figures helps you read between the lines and write with more color.",
