    tip: "Cover the rest of the sentence and test the first part. Before a semicolon, both sides should stand alone as sentences. Before a colon, the part before it should stand alone. For a hyphen, ask whether the words describe the noun together: *a part-time job* is a job that is part-time.",
    note: "American style puts periods and commas inside closing quotation marks (*“Yes,” she said*); British style often puts them outside unless they belong to the quotation. Most American books write the em dash with no spaces (*now—or never*), though American newspapers put a space on each side. After a colon, some American guides capitalize a complete sentence and others don't; pick one and be consistent.",
    myth: "“Semicolons are old-fashioned, so avoid them.” Some famous writers have disliked them—Kurt Vonnegut joked that all they do is show you've been to college—but semicolons are still standard in academic, legal and business writing. Used correctly and not too often, they show a close link between two ideas.",
    fact: "The semicolon first appeared in print in Venice in the 1490s, in a short book by the scholar Pietro Bembo, published by the printer Aldus Manutius. The same printer's shop later introduced italic type, the slanted letters this course uses for the English words it talks about.",
    checks: [
      { q: "How should you join “I'm not hungry” and “I had a big lunch”?", choices: ["I'm not hungry, I had a big lunch.", "I'm not hungry; I had a big lunch.", "I'm not hungry; and I had a big lunch."], answer: 1, why: "Two related full sentences can be joined with a semicolon." },
      { q: "Which sentence about the packing list uses a colon correctly?", choices: ["Pack: a toothbrush, socks and a charger.", "Pack the: toothbrush, socks and charger.", "Pack the essentials: a toothbrush, socks and a charger."], answer: 2, why: "A complete sentence, *Pack the essentials*, comes before the colon." },
      { q: "Which sentence about Ines's job is spelled correctly?", choices: ["Ines has a full-time job.", "Ines has a full time-job.", "Ines has a fulltime-job."], answer: 0, why: "*Full-time* is one adjective before the noun, so the hyphen joins *full* and *time*." },
      { q: "My daughter is ___ old.", choices: ["six-years", "six years", "six-year"], answer: 1, why: "After the verb, no hyphen and a plural: *six years old*." },
      { q: "We just adopted a ___ puppy.", choices: ["two-months-old", "two-month-old"], answer: 1, why: "Before a noun: hyphens and the singular *month*." },
      { q: "How do you write 47 in words?", choices: ["fourty-seven", "four-seven", "forty-seven"], answer: 2, why: "Compound numbers take a hyphen, and *forty* has no *u*." },
      { q: "Which sentence about the semester uses a semicolon correctly?", choices: ["The semester ends in May; then I start my internship.", "The semester ends in May; and my internship.", "The semester ends; in May."], answer: 0, why: "Both sides of the semicolon are complete sentences." },
      { q: "Why are there semicolons in “We met Ana, the designer; Raj, the editor; and Lee, the writer”?", choices: ["They join two sentences.", "The list items already contain commas.", "They introduce a quotation."], answer: 1, why: "Semicolons separate list items that have commas inside them." },
      { q: "Her text message said *I'm on my way*. Which version is punctuated the American way?", choices: ["“I'm on my way”, she texted.", "“I'm on my way,” she texted."], answer: 1, why: "American style puts the comma inside the quotation marks." },
      { q: "What does the pair of dashes do in “The plan—at least the first part—worked”?", choices: ["shows a list", "joins two words into an adjective", "sets off an interruption"], answer: 2, why: "Two dashes set off an extra thought in the middle of a sentence." },
      { q: "In which sentence is *well known* correct without a hyphen?", choices: ["The speaker is well known.", "She is a well known speaker."], answer: 0, why: "After the noun and a verb, *well known* usually has no hyphen." },
      { q: "What do the parentheses show in “The hotel (the one near the station) was full”?", choices: ["a quotation", "extra, less important information", "the main point"], answer: 1, why: "Parentheses hold information the sentence could do without." },
      { q: "What's wrong with “My goals are: to save money and travel”?", choices: ["*Goals* needs a hyphen.", "It needs a semicolon after *money*.", "The colon comes after an incomplete sentence."], answer: 2, why: "*My goals are* can't stand alone, so drop the colon." },
      { q: "Which sentence about the soccer game is a comma splice?", choices: ["We lost the game, but we played well.", "We lost the game, we played well.", "We lost the game; we played well."], answer: 1, why: "Two full sentences joined only by a comma make a comma splice." },
      { q: "Which sentence about the weekend is punctuated correctly?", choices: ["We have two options: stay home or visit Grandma.", "We have: two options stay home or visit Grandma.", "We have two options; stay home or visit Grandma."], answer: 0, why: "A complete sentence, then a colon to introduce the options." },
      { q: "In “a ten-dollar bill,” why is there no *s* on *dollar*?", choices: ["Money words never take *-s*.", "It's a spelling mistake.", "Words in a hyphenated adjective before a noun are usually singular."], answer: 2, why: "Compare *ten dollars* with *a ten-dollar bill*." },
      { q: "Which sentence about Mateo's playlist punctuates the titles the American way?", choices: ["My favorite song on the album “Blue Skies” is *Morning Light*.", "My favorite song on the album Blue Skies is Morning Light.", "My favorite song on the album *Blue Skies* is “Morning Light.”"], answer: 2, why: "An album, a whole work, is in italics; a song, one part of it, goes in quotation marks." },
      { q: "You quote “The new library, which cost more than planned, opens in June” but leave out the middle part. How do you show the missing words?", choices: ["“The new library [opens in June].”", "“The new library … opens in June.”", "“The new library, opens in June.”"], answer: 1, why: "An *ellipsis* (…) marks words left out of a quotation." },
      { q: "In “He [the coach] praised every player,” why are there square brackets?", choices: ["The writer added words to make the quotation clear.", "The words are less important, like a note in parentheses.", "*The coach* is a title."], answer: 0, why: "Brackets mark words the writer added to someone else's quotation." },
      { q: "Listen. What number do you hear?", say: "My grandmother just turned ninety-two.", choices: ["twenty-nine", "ninety-two", "ninety"], answer: 1, why: "*Ninety-two*: a compound number, written with a hyphen." },
      { q: "Listen. How old is the building?", say: "We live in a two-hundred-year-old building.", choices: ["two hundred years old", "twenty years old", "two years old"], answer: 0, why: "*A two-hundred-year-old building* is two hundred years old." },
      { q: "Listen. How many people did they meet?", say: "We met Mia, a nurse; Omar, a chef; and Jun, a pilot.", choices: ["six", "two", "three"], answer: 2, why: "Three people, each with a job; in writing, semicolons separate them." },
      { q: "Listen. What did the speaker forget?", say: "I forgot only one thing: my keys.", choices: ["my phone", "my keys", "my wallet"], answer: 1, why: "The part after the colon names the one thing: *my keys*." },
      { q: "Which sentence about the new apartment is punctuated correctly?", choices: ["The apartment is small, however, the view is amazing.", "The apartment is small however; the view is amazing.", "The apartment is small; however, the view is amazing."], answer: 2, why: "A semicolon before *however* and a comma after it." },
      { q: "Which sentence about the job offer is punctuated correctly?", choices: ["She got the offer—a full-time position in Denver.", "She got the offer: a full time-position in Denver.", "She got the offer; a full-time position in Denver."], answer: 0, why: "The dash adds emphasis; a semicolon needs a full sentence after it." },
    ],
